WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:04.100
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:10.820
When I was growing up, IQ tests were created
by humans to test the intelligence of other

00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:11.820
humans.

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:17.660
If someone told me just 10 years ago that
algorithms will create IQ tests to be taken

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:21.800
by other algorithms, I wouldn’t have believed
a word of it.

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:28.390
Yet, just a year ago, scientists at DeepMind
created a program that is able to generate

00:00:28.390 --> 00:00:32.320
a large amount of problems that test abstract
reasoning capabilities.

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:39.070
They are inspired by human IQ-tests with all
the these questions about sizes, colors and

00:00:39.070 --> 00:00:40.070
progressions.

00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:45.110
And then, they wrote their own neural network
to take these tests, which performed remarkably

00:00:45.110 --> 00:00:46.110
well.

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:47.379
How well exactly?

00:00:47.379 --> 00:00:52.809
In the presence of nasty distractor objects,
it was able to find out the correct solution

00:00:52.809 --> 00:00:59.360
about 62% of the time, and, if we removed
these distractors, which, I will note that

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:05.850
are good at misdirecting humans too, the AI
was correct 78% of the time!

00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:06.850
Awesome.

00:01:06.850 --> 00:01:11.830
But today, we are capable of writing even
more sophisticated learning algorithms that

00:01:11.830 --> 00:01:14.609
can even complete our sentences!

00:01:14.609 --> 00:01:20.469
Not so long ago, the OpenAI lab published
GPT-2, a technique that they unleashed to

00:01:20.469 --> 00:01:24.200
read the internet, and it learned our language
by itself.

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:29.880
A few episodes ago, we gave it a spin, and
I almost fell out of the chair when I saw

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:35.130
that it could finish my sentences about fluid
simulations in such a scholarly way, that

00:01:35.130 --> 00:01:38.430
I think, could easily fool a layperson.

00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:40.659
Have a look here and judge for yourself!

00:01:40.659 --> 00:01:46.929
This GPT-2 technique was a neural network
variant that was trained using 1.5 billion

00:01:46.929 --> 00:01:48.109
parameters.

00:01:48.109 --> 00:01:53.149
At the risk of oversimplifying what that means,
it roughly refers to the internal complexity

00:01:53.149 --> 00:01:58.820
of the networks, or in other words, how many
weights and connections are there.

00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:05.719
And now, the Google Brain team has released
Meena, an open-domain chatbot that uses 2.6

00:02:05.719 --> 00:02:10.640
billion parameters, and shows remarkable human-like
properties.

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:15.909
The chatbot part means a piece of software
or a machine that we can talk to, and the

00:02:15.909 --> 00:02:23.269
open-domain part refers to the fact that we
can try any topic, hotels, movies, the ocean,

00:02:23.269 --> 00:02:28.739
favorite movie characters, or pretty much
anything we can think of and expect the bot

00:02:28.739 --> 00:02:30.030
to do well.

00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:32.299
So how do we know that it’s really good?

00:02:32.299 --> 00:02:36.099
Well, let’s try to evaluate it in two different
ways.

00:02:36.099 --> 00:02:41.780
First, let’s try the super fun, but less
scientific way, or, in other words, what we

00:02:41.780 --> 00:02:45.030
are already doing, looking at chat logs!

00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:49.670
You see a Meena writing on the left, and a
human being on the right, and it not only

00:02:49.670 --> 00:02:56.799
answers questions sensibly and coherently,
but is even capable of cracking a joke.

00:02:56.799 --> 00:03:01.760
Of course, if you consider a pun to be a joke,
that is.

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:06.550
You see a selection of topics here, where
the user talks with Meena about movies, and

00:03:06.550 --> 00:03:13.030
the bot expresses the desire to see The Grand
Budapest Hotel, which is indeed a very humanlike

00:03:13.030 --> 00:03:14.690
quality.

00:03:14.690 --> 00:03:19.299
It can also try to come up with a proper definition
of philosophy.

00:03:19.299 --> 00:03:24.499
And now, since we are scholars, we would also
like to measure how humanlike this is in a

00:03:24.499 --> 00:03:26.829
more scientific manner as well!

00:03:26.829 --> 00:03:32.109
Now is a good time to hold on to your papers,
because this is measured with the Sensibleness

00:03:32.109 --> 00:03:39.159
and Specificity Average score, from now on,
SSA in short, in which, humans are here, previous

00:03:39.159 --> 00:03:45.829
chatbots are down there, and Meena is right
there, close by, which means that it is easy

00:03:45.829 --> 00:03:48.609
to be confused for a real human.

00:03:48.609 --> 00:03:53.780
That already sounds like science fiction,
however, let’s be a little nosy here and

00:03:53.780 --> 00:04:00.889
also ask, how do we know that this SSA is
any good in predicting what is humanlike and

00:04:00.889 --> 00:04:02.359
what isn’t?

00:04:02.359 --> 00:04:03.849
Excellent question.

00:04:03.849 --> 00:04:09.329
When measuring human-likeness for these chatbots,
plugging in this SSA, again, the Sensibleness

00:04:09.329 --> 00:04:14.439
and Specificity Average, we see that they
correlate really strongly, which means that

00:04:14.439 --> 00:04:20.730
the two seem to measure very similar things,
and in this case, SSA can indeed be used as

00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:23.380
a proxy for human likeness.

00:04:23.380 --> 00:04:25.870
The coefficient of determination is 0.96.

00:04:25.870 --> 00:04:31.199
To put this into perspective, this is a several
times stronger correlation than we can measure

00:04:31.199 --> 00:04:37.199
between the intelligence and the grades of
a student, which is already a great correlation.

00:04:37.199 --> 00:04:39.300
This is a remarkable result.

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:45.500
Now, what we get out of this is that the SSA
is much easier and precise to measure than

00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:48.650
human likeness, and is hence, used throughout
the paper.

00:04:48.650 --> 00:04:50.389
So, chatbots eh?

00:04:50.389 --> 00:04:53.320
What are all these things useful for?

00:04:53.320 --> 00:04:59.150
Well, remember Google’s technique that would
automatically use an AI to talk to your callers

00:04:59.150 --> 00:05:01.370
and screen your calls?

00:05:01.370 --> 00:05:04.240
Or even make calls on your behalf.

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:09.520
When connected to a text to speech synthesizer,
something that Google already does amazingly

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:13.099
well, Meena could really come alive in our
daily lives soon.

00:05:13.099 --> 00:05:16.060
What a time to be alive!

